For Google DeepMind L5, API Gateway is a very good HLD topic 
      because the interviewer is not primarily testing whether you know what an API Gateway is. 

They want to see whether you can reason about 
      traffic, 
      security, 
      scalability, 
      failure isolation, 
      rate limiting, 
      routing, 
      and trade-offs.
===============================================================================================================================================
I'll build it from first principles.

API Gateway — HLD for Google DeepMind L5

1. First: What problem are we solving?

                Imagine we have a microservices system:
                                         Clients
                                            |
                          +-----------------+-----------------+
                          |                 |                 |
                       Mobile             Web             External
                          |                 |                 |
                          v                 v                 v
                
                       User Service     Order Service    Payment Service
                       Product Service  Search Service   Notification Service


If clients directly call every microservice, we have a problem.

A mobile application would need to know:
              user-service.company.com
              order-service.company.com
              payment-service.company.com
              search-service.company.com
              ...

And every service would need to independently handle:
                authentication
                authorization
                rate limiting
                logging
                TLS
                request validation
                routing
                API versioning
                abuse protection

That's a lot of duplication.

So we introduce:

                         Clients
                            |
                            v
                    +---------------+
                    | API GATEWAY   |
                    +---------------+
                       /    |     \
                      /     |      \
                     v      v       v
                  User   Order    Payment
                 Service Service  Service



The gateway becomes the controlled entry point into the backend system.

2. But why do we need a Gateway?
              This is the question I would expect from an L5 interviewer:
              
                          "Why can't the Load Balancer do this?"              
              Excellent question.
              
              A Load Balancer and API Gateway solve related but different problems.
              
              Load Balancer              
                    Primarily answers:                    
                              "Which healthy server should receive this request?"
              
              For example:
                             Load Balancer
                            /      |       \
                           v       v        v
                        Server1 Server2  Server3

              API Gateway              
                    Answers:                    
                            "Is this request allowed, what API does it target, what policies apply, and which backend should handle it?"
              
              For example:
                            Client
                              |
                              v
                            API Gateway
                              |
                              +---- authentication
                              |
                              +---- authorization
                              |
                              +---- rate limit
                              |
                              +---- API version
                              |
                              +---- routing
                              |
                              v
                            Backend

The gateway is therefore more of an API policy and traffic-management layer, not merely a server-selection mechanism.

Google's API Gateway documentation similarly describes the gateway as providing secure access 
to backend services and handling routing/authentication before forwarding requests.
===============================================================================================================================================
3. What responsibilities should an API Gateway have?

I would group them into six categories.

                    API GATEWAY
                         |
       +-----------------+------------------+
       |                 |                  |
 Authentication     Traffic Control      Routing
       |                 |                  |
       +-----------------+------------------+
                         |
                  API Management
                         |
             +-----------+-----------+
             |                       |
          Security              Observability

1.  Authentication:
^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

                  Suppose the client sends:                  
                          GET /users/123
                          Authorization: Bearer eyJ...
                  
                  The gateway can validate the token.
                  
                  Conceptually:
                                Client
                                  |
                                  | JWT
                                  v
                                API Gateway
                                  |
                                  | Is JWT valid?
                                  |
                                  +---- NO ----> 401
                                  |
                                  +---- YES
                                         |
                                         v
                                      Backend

Why do this at the gateway?
        Because otherwise every microservice has to implement:
                JWT parsing
                signature verification
                expiry validation
                issuer validation
                audience validation
          independently.

        Centralizing common authentication reduces duplication.

        Google's API Gateway, for example, 
        supports JWT validation and validates claims such as issuer/audience before forwarding requests.

        But important L5 nuance
              Authentication at the gateway does NOT mean the backend should blindly trust any request.

        The backend should still enforce authorization for sensitive operations.

For example:
          Gateway:
                  "Is this Alice?"
          
          Backend:
                  "Is Alice allowed to delete this particular account?"

Authentication and authorization are different.

5. Authorization:
   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ 
          Suppose:          
                  DELETE /users/123
          
          The gateway knows:
                            user = Alice
                            role = USER
          
                  But Alice might not have permission to delete arbitrary users.

      So:
          Authentication
                ↓
          Who are you?
          
                                Authorization
                                      ↓
                                What are you allowed to do?

      Some coarse-grained authorization can happen at the gateway:
      
                      /admin/*
      
                    requires:  ADMIN
      
      But business-specific authorization should generally remain in the backend.

6. Rate Limiting:
   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
              This is one of the most important API Gateway responsibilities.
              
              Imagine a client sends:    10,000 requests/sec
                                          to:                                          
                                          POST /payment
                                          
                                If we allow everything through:
                                          Client
                                            |
                                            | 10,000 req/s
                                            v
                                          Gateway
                                            |
                                            | 10,000 req/s
                                            v
                                          Payment Service
                                            |
                                            X
                                          OVERLOADED


                      Instead:
                                          Client
                                            |
                                            | 10,000 req/s
                                            v
                                          Gateway
                                            |
                                            | rate limit = 100 req/s
                                            |
                                            +---- 100 → Backend
                                            |
                                            +---- 9,900 → 429
                      The backend is protected.
===============================================================================================================================================
7. Why rate limiting belongs near the edge

                    Imagine the request travels:
                    
                                                Internet
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                                Load Balancer
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                                Gateway
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                                20 microservices
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                                Database
                                                
                      If we discover the client is abusing the system only at the database level, 
                         we've already consumed a lot of resources.
                                                
                      We want to reject bad/excessive traffic as early as possible.                                          
                                          Internet
                                             |
                                             v
                                          Gateway
                                             |
                                             X reject

                  That's much cheaper.

Google's API Gateway documentation describes quotas as a mechanism to prevent excessive callers 
from negatively affecting other API consumers and the overall health of the API
===============================================================================================================================================
8. But how do we rate-limit?

                There are several algorithms.                
                For L5, you should know at least:
                
                1.Fixed Window :
                  **************
                            For example:                            
                                        100 requests / minute
                            
                            At:                            
                                12:00:00 → 12:00:59                
                            allow 100.
                
                            Then reset.

Problem: boundary burst.
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
                A client could send:                
                        100 requests at 12:00:59
                        100 requests at 12:01:00
                
                That's 200 requests almost instantly.

            2. Sliding Window:
              ******************
                              Instead of fixed calendar windows, look at a rolling period.
                              
                              For example:                              
                                    Last 60 seconds.
                              
                              More accurate, but maintaining the request history is more expensive.



              3.Token Bucket:
              ****************
                  This is particularly useful for APIs.
                  
                  Imagine a bucket containing tokens.                  
                        Bucket capacity = 100
                        Refill = 10 tokens/sec
                  
                  Every request consumes one token.

                                Request
                                   |
                                   v
                                Token available?
                                   |
                                 +---+---+
                                 |       |
                                YES      NO
                                 |       |
                                 v       v
                                Allow   Reject

                The nice property is that it allows controlled bursts.
                
                      For example:                
                                  Bucket has 100 tokens
                                  
                                  Client sends 20 requests quickly
                                  
                                  20 tokens consumed
                                  
                                  80 remain
                
                Then tokens gradually refill.

            4. Distributed Rate Limiting
               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
                              Now comes the real HLD question.
                              
                              Suppose we have:

                                                 Gateway Cluster
                                            +---------+---------+
                                            |         |         |
                                           G1        G2        G3

                              If the rate limit is:  100 requests/sec/user
                              
                              we cannot independently maintain:                              
                                                G1 → 100
                                                G2 → 100
                                                G3 → 100
                              
                              because the user could effectively get:  300 requests/sec
                                    
                                    We need shared state or a distributed quota mechanism.
                                    
                              Conceptually:
                                    
                                                      G1 ──┐
                                                      G2 ──┼──> Distributed Rate Limit State
                                                      G3 ──┘
                                    
                              Possible implementations include a distributed in-memory store or a dedicated quota service.
                                    
                              This is where the L5 discussion becomes interesting:                                    
                                    How do you balance accuracy of global rate limits against the latency 
                                          and availability cost of centralized coordination?
                                    
                              You don't necessarily need perfect global precision.

                        5. Routing
                           ^^^^^^^^       
                                    Suppose the client calls:  GET /api/v1/users/123
                                    
                                    Gateway determines:  /api/v1/users/*
                                                                    ↓
                                                            User Service
                                    
                                    And: POST /api/v1/orders
                                    
                                    goes to: /api/v1/orders/*
                                                        ↓
                                                Order Service
                                    
                                    So:                                    
                                                        Gateway
                                                           |
                                            +--------------+--------------+
                                            |              |              |
                                            v              v              v
                                       /users/*        /orders/*      /payments/*
                                            |              |              |
                                            v              v              v
                                          User           Order         Payment


===============================================================================================================================================
13. Why is routing useful?
                  It means clients don't need to know the internal service topology.
                  
                  Without gateway:
                  
                              Mobile App
                                 |
                                 +--> user-service-17.internal
                                 +--> order-service-32.internal
                                 +--> payment-service-9.internal

                  That's bad.
                  
                  With gateway:
                  
                  Mobile App
                      |
                      v
                  api.company.com
                      |
                      +--> User
                      +--> Order
                      +--> Payment
                  
                  The internal architecture can evolve without forcing clients to change.
                  
                  That's an important form of abstraction.
==================================================================================================================================
  14. API Versioning
                  
            Suppose we currently have:   /api/v1/orders
                  
            Then we introduce: /api/v2/orders
                  
            The gateway can route:  /api/v1/orders → Order Service V1                  
                                    /api/v2/orders → Order Service V2
                  
           This allows gradual migration.
                  
                  Clients
                     |
                     v
                  Gateway
                     |
                     +---- V1 → Old backend
                     |
                     +---- V2 → New backend
                  
            Eventually:  V1 traffic → 0                  
                         Then we retire V1.
